@local/dsh-telegram-bridge README · Telegram Bridge for DeepSeek Harness：No inbound port, no webhook, no public URL: the plugin long-polls the Telegram
Bot API from the Host.

---

@local/dsh-telegram-bridge README · 1. Create a bot and get a token：1. Open Telegram, talk to [@BotFather](https://t.me/BotFather), send `/newbot`.
2. Choose a name and a username (`…_bot`).
3. Copy the token (`123456789:AA…`).

@local/dsh-telegram-bridge README · Session buttons：The session card (`/status`, after selecting a session, and on push
notifications) has Result / Review / Sessions / Stop / Refresh / Notifications
plus **⚙️ All controls**. That opens one card with every action:

@local/dsh-telegram-bridge README · Session buttons：Approval and question messages carry **inline buttons**; questions with multiple
selectable answers also get a **✅ Done** button and an **✏️ Other** button that
switches the conversation into "type your answer" mode for that question.

@local/dsh-telegram-bridge README · Routing a reply to the right session：The mapping is remembered in memory (24 h, up to 300 messages per chat). After a
restart, replies to older messages fall back to the active session, so send
`/sessions` or tap the notification's buttons to be explicit.

@local/dsh-telegram-bridge README · Telling the model the user is remote：A DSH session normally belongs to someone sitting in front of the desktop app, so
the agent will happily open files/windows there unless told otherwise. The bridge
makes the transport explicit:

@local/dsh-telegram-bridge README · Telling the model the user is remote：The marker stays in the session log next to the request (visible if you later
read that session in the desktop app), which keeps the origin durable across
resume and forks. Prompts typed directly in the DSH app have no marker.

@local/dsh-telegram-bridge README · Notifications：**Origin-aware pushes.** Every Telegram prompt starts with `[via Telegram]`, so
